{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Current state — THIS MACHINE IS RUNNING WITHOUT AUTO-UPDATES\nThe hourly main-tracking updater was PAUSED on 2026-08-20 by operator decision, to stop it breaking\nin-flight work:\n\n```\nlaunchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.navapbc.rebar-dev-update      # what was run\nlaunchctl list | grep rebar-dev-update                           # now returns nothing\n```\n\nUntil this ticket is resolved, the global `rebar`/`rebar-mcp` on this host **no longer tracks\n`origin/main`** and will drift. That is a deliberate, temporary trade — but it is a standing\nregression in the environment, and this ticket owns undoing it safely.\n\n## The defect\n`~/.local/bin/rebar-dev-update.sh` (host-local, NOT in this repository) ends every run — the publish\npath AND the no-change path — with `prune_releases()`, which unconditionally `rm -rf`s every release\ndirectory that `current` does not point at, with no check for live processes.\n\nThe published runtime is an EDITABLE install: the release venv's site-packages contains only\n`_editable_impl_nava_rebar.pth` holding `<release>/src`, so a running interpreter resolves\nnot-yet-imported modules from that directory AT IMPORT TIME. rebar also imports lazily — the\nconfirmed case is `commit_impact` at `_commands/close_precheck.py:283`, inside\n`_check_file_impact_vs_diff`, which runs LATE in a close, after the minutes-long completion-verifier\nLLM gate. That is a multi-minute window in which the backing directory can vanish.\n\n## Evidence — two independent in-the-wild occurrences\n1. **2026-08-19**, original report, traced on `frangible-irongray-adouri` (closed as a duplicate of\n   `shady-blueblack-whale`). Log timeline: `d83fb4f6` published 17:25:18, `0b851c81` published\n   19:18:01 pruning `d83fb4f6`, failure reported 19:55:13 quoting a traceback rooted at the deleted\n   path. Reproduced offline character-for-character: import the parent package, `rmtree` the tree,\n   trigger the lazy import; negative control with the tree intact imports fine.\n2. **2026-08-20**, during this session, on a DIFFERENT release hash. A `rebar transition … closed`\n   died with\n   `ImportError: cannot import name 'commit_impact' from 'rebar._engine_support'` naming\n   `.../releases/d6841983c6…84448/…`. State captured immediately after: `releases/` contained\n   exactly ONE directory, `b91f8f6830…86589`; `current` pointed at it; the path in the traceback was\n   gone. The close FAILED and left the ticket `in_progress`, and a naive caller reading the exit\n   code would have believed it succeeded. Retrying against the fresh release then worked.\n\nNote both occurrences involved NO `uv tool install --force` — an ordinary scheduled prune is\nsufficient. That matters for `shady-blueblack-whale`, which is tracing the same class from the uv\ntrigger; a reproduction harness can be driven by a plain directory removal.\n\n## The hard constraint — why the obvious fixes are wrong\nThis host uses rebar **heavily and continually**: multiple concurrent agents, each running gates and\ncloses that take minutes. Any remedy must survive that, and the two naive options both fail:\n\n* **\"Never prune a release with live referencing processes.\"** Under continuous use there may never\n  be a moment with no live process, so releases accumulate without bound.\n* **\"Prune aggressively, accept the breakage.\"** That is today's behaviour, and it silently fails\n  closes.\n\nAnd unbounded accumulation is not hypothetical here: `masonic-abeyant-stagbeetle` (P1, closed\n2026-08-19) was a root-disk-pressure incident on the review-bot host, where the only thing bounding a\ngrowing store failed to engage until 93.3% disk used. A retention policy that never reclaims\nrecreates that incident. **The remedy must both stop deleting live releases AND keep reclaiming\nspace under continuous load.** Solving one at the expense of the other is not a fix.\n\nNote also that making the install non-editable does NOT by itself solve this: the prune deletes the\nwhole release directory including its `.venv`, so a copied package inside it disappears too. The\nproblem is deleting a directory a live process is executing from, not the editable indirection\nspecifically.\n\n## Candidate approaches (to be chosen on evidence, not assumed)\n- **Bounded retention**: keep the previous N releases (or any younger than T), so a running process\n  always has a grace window, with N/T sized against the longest realistic operation (a close with the\n  LLM completion verifier — measure it rather than guess).\n- **Liveness check with a bounded override**: skip pruning a release with live referencing processes,\n  but cap total retained releases/bytes; on breaching the cap, ALERT rather than delete a live\n  release, so the failure mode is a warning instead of a broken run.\n- **Publish-time indirection**: have the launcher resolve and pin a release for the process lifetime\n  so a prune cannot invalidate an in-flight resolution.\n- Some combination; defence in depth is acceptable if each layer is justified.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] [non-codebase] **The updater is RE-ENABLED on this host and confirmed running** — `launchctl list` shows\n      `com.navapbc.rebar-dev-update` again, and a forced run\n      (`launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.navapbc.rebar-dev-update`) completes successfully.\n      This ticket is not done while the host is still drifting from `origin/main`.\n- [x] [non-codebase] A long-running `rebar` operation spanning at least one updater cycle COMPLETES successfully\n      while the updater is active — demonstrated, not argued. The natural probe is a real close\n      (whose LLM completion verifier takes minutes) with a forced update fired mid-run.\n- [x] [non-codebase] Disk usage remains BOUNDED under continuous rebar use: releases do not accumulate without\n      limit when operations are always in flight. State the bound (count or bytes) and show it\n      holding, so this does not recreate the `masonic-abeyant-stagbeetle` disk-pressure incident.\n- [x] [non-codebase] When the two goals genuinely conflict — a release cannot be reclaimed because it is in use and\n      the retention bound is reached — the behaviour is an explicit ALERT, not a silent deletion and\n      not a silent unbounded grow. Name where that alert surfaces.\n- [x] [non-codebase] The chosen mechanism and its rationale are documented in\n      `docs/local-env-main-tracking.local.md` (git-ignored, host-local), including how to pause and\n      resume, so the next operator does not rediscover this.\n- [x] [non-codebase] The failure signature is recorded somewhere a future reader will find it: an `ImportError`\n      naming a module that demonstrably exists in the source tree, with a path under\n      `~/.local/state/rebar-dev/releases/` that no longer exists, means a pruned-mid-run release —\n      not a packaging defect. Two sessions have now mis-diagnosed this as a packaging bug.\n\n## Scope note\nThe defective script `~/.local/bin/rebar-dev-update.sh` is HOST-LOCAL and outside this repository, so\nthe fix may land with no repo file impact. If investigation shows rebar itself should participate\n(for example a launcher that pins a resolved release for the process lifetime), that is a distinct\nin-repo change and should be filed separately rather than absorbed here.\n\nRelated: `shady-blueblack-whale` traces the same mechanism under the `uv tool install --force`\ntrigger; `frangible-irongray-adouri` (closed duplicate) carries the original traced RCA;\n`masonic-abeyant-stagbeetle` is the disk-pressure incident that constrains the retention side.\n\n\n---\n\n## Plan (2026-08-20) — three-layer prune safety: bounded retention + flock liveness pinning + cap-with-alert\n\n### Approach and rationale\nThe host runs multiple concurrent sessions continuously with no reliable quiet windows\n(operator-confirmed), so a liveness-only rule can never guarantee reclamation and a\nretention-only rule cannot protect long-lived processes (`rebar-mcp` servers have survived\nfor days across updates — see 2391-a941-69ff-4645). The remedy is three cooperating layers\nin `~/.local/bin/rebar-dev-update.sh`, chosen against the prior art this layout copies\n(Capistrano `releases/` + `current`, whose standard answer is `keep_releases` bounded\nretention) plus a deterministic liveness signal:\n\n1. **Bounded retention (grace window).** `prune_releases()` never deletes the `current`\n   target NOR any of the newest **N=3** releases by publish order. At the hourly cadence\n   that is a >=2h grace window; the longest normal CLI operation (a close incl. the LLM\n   completion verifier) is minutes, so N=3 covers it with an order-of-magnitude margin.\n2. **flock liveness pinning (covers long-lived processes).** The stable shims\n   `~/.local/bin/rebar` and `~/.local/bin/rebar-mcp` change from plain symlinks to small\n   bash wrappers (installed idempotently by `ensure_stable_links`). A wrapper: resolves the\n   `current` target, opens an fd on `<release>/.inuse` (creating it if absent), takes a\n   **shared non-blocking flock** on that fd (host has flock(1); operator-confirmed), then\n   `exec`s the release entrypoint with the fd inherited — the lock therefore lives exactly\n   as long as the process tree. If the release vanishes or the shared lock is refused\n   (pruner holds exclusive), the wrapper re-resolves `current` once and retries; a second\n   failure is a hard error naming this ticket. The pruner, before deleting any candidate,\n   takes an **exclusive non-blocking flock** on that release's `.inuse` and deletes only\n   while holding it — lock refused means a live process, skip. This closes the TOCTOU race\n   deterministically (no lsof heuristics, no PID scans). A missing `.inuse` (pre-wrapper\n   release) is treated as not-live; layer 1 still protects it.\n3. **Cap + alert, never silent (disk bound).** Hard cap **M=10** release directories\n   (~3.5 GB at the measured 346 MB/release; disk is 1.8 TB with 740 GB free — the bound is\n   the count, stated per the masonic-abeyant-stagbeetle constraint). If, after pruning\n   everything layer 1+2 permit, more than M releases remain (i.e. live locks block\n   reclamation), the updater does NOT delete a live release and instead publishes an alert\n   to **SNS topic `arn:aws:sns:us-east-1:896586841071:rebar-gerrit-alerts`** (the\n   terraform-managed alerts topic with the email subscription to the operator; publish\n   access from this host verified 2026-08-20, MessageId bdc90c6e-d1b1-5903-9364-6f6fcc4c8a6b).\n   Alerts are throttled to one per condition per 24h via a state file under\n   `~/.local/state/rebar-dev/` to avoid hourly spam. An `aws sns publish` failure is logged\n   as ERROR to `update.log` (residual risk: the alert path cannot alert about itself; the\n   throttle file makes the condition greppable). The same channel also fires after **3\n   consecutive candidate rejections**, so the 43-run silent-pin failure mode\n   (b477339a99 incident recorded in the script header) becomes visible.\n\n**Alternatives rejected:** liveness-only (unbounded growth under continuous load —\nrecreates masonic-abeyant-stagbeetle); retention-only (does not cover multi-day `rebar-mcp`\nprocesses); non-editable install (prune deletes the release's whole `.venv`, so copied\npackages vanish identically); lsof-based liveness (heuristic — venv interpreters/entry\nscripts are not reliably held open, and it is racy against process start).\n\n### Edge cases\n- **Wrapper vs pruner race:** resolved by lock ordering above (pruner deletes only while\n  holding the exclusive lock; wrapper re-resolves on refusal).\n- **Long-lived `rebar-mcp` pinning a release for days:** intended behaviour — it counts\n  toward M and triggers the alert if it blocks reclamation; the operator restarts the\n  session. No hot-reload is attempted.\n- **Legacy `releases/legacy` dir and pre-wrapper releases:** no `.inuse` → not-live;\n  newest-N still applies; `current` target always kept.\n- **`aws` CLI absent from launchd PATH:** the script already pins PATH; the alert helper\n  resolves the aws binary explicitly and logs ERROR if unavailable.\n- **Updater crash mid-prune:** deletion happens per-release under its own lock; a crash\n  strands at most already-dead directories, reclaimed on the next hourly run (idempotent).\n\n### Execution steps\n1. Modify `~/.local/bin/rebar-dev-update.sh`: rewrite `prune_releases()` (newest-N + flock\n   check + cap count + alert call), extend `ensure_stable_links()` to install the two\n   wrapper scripts (idempotent, atomic via the existing staged-rename helper), add the\n   alert/throttle helper and rejection-streak counter. Keep the existing\n   `REBAR_DEV_TEST_FAIL_STAGE` pattern and add `REBAR_DEV_ALERT_CMD` (test seam: capture\n   the publish invocation instead of calling AWS).\n2. Verify offline (see oracles below) with the updater still paused.\n3. Re-enable: `launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.navapbc.rebar-dev-update.plist`,\n   then `launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.navapbc.rebar-dev-update`; confirm\n   `launchctl list` shows the label and `update.log` shows a successful run.\n4. Write `docs/local-env-main-tracking.local.md` (git-ignored, host-local): mechanism,\n   N/M values and rationale, pause/resume commands, alert channel + throttle, and the\n   failure signature (\"ImportError naming a module that exists in the source tree, path\n   under ~/.local/state/rebar-dev/releases/ that no longer exists = pruned-mid-run\n   release, not a packaging defect\").\n5. Run the live probe (AC oracle 2 below), close this ticket.\n\n### Verification oracles (tier per test-design standard)\n1. **Prune safety under load** — tier: multiple real processes with a deterministic\n   barrier. A background process starts through the real wrapper (so it holds the shared\n   flock) and signals readiness via a marker file; the pruner then runs with N=0 forced;\n   assert the locked release directory still exists and an unlocked stale sibling was\n   deleted (contrast control). Precondition proven by asserting the marker + lock state\n   first.\n2. **Live operation spans an update cycle** — tier: real CLI subprocess, the AC's natural\n   probe: a real `rebar transition … closed` (minutes-long completion verifier) with\n   `launchctl kickstart -k` fired mid-run; oracle: the close exits 0 and the ticket lands\n   `closed` (this ticket's own close is the probe).\n3. **Bounded disk** — seed >M stale unlocked release dirs; run the updater once; assert\n   count(releases) <= max(N+1, live) and state the byte bound (M x 346 MB).\n4. **Cap-breach alerts, never deletes live** — seed M+1 flock-held releases with\n   `REBAR_DEV_ALERT_CMD` capturing; run prune; assert the capture file contains the\n   publish (topic ARN + condition) and every locked release survives. Negative control:\n   same seed unlocked → pruned, no alert.\n5. **Re-enabled** — `launchctl list | grep com.navapbc.rebar-dev-update` non-empty and a\n   kickstarted run logs success (AC 1).\nAll oracles run in-session from this host (the mechanism is host-local; there is no CI\ntier for it — the updater is exactly the machinery CI cannot see, and the ticket's scope\nnote already establishes host-local residency).\n\n### Scope, compat, rollback\nNo repository file impact: the changes are `~/.local/bin/rebar-dev-update.sh`, the two\nshim wrappers, and a git-ignored host-local doc. Rollback = restore the prior script from\nthe backup copy taken before editing (`rebar-dev-update.sh.pre-df15`) and re-run\n`ensure_stable_links` from it (restores plain symlinks); pause remains\n`launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.navapbc.rebar-dev-update`, as today. No in-repo\nlauncher change is needed (the wrapper achieves publish-time pinning host-locally), so no\nfollow-up repo ticket is filed unless verification falsifies that.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787240773805540001,"uuid":"d91e02b9-4393-4323-af30-fb7d511d990e"}